[Music]  on the dish this morning the korean  vegan joanne lee molinaro was a  high-profile trial lawyer until  transitioning to cooking videos on  youtube and tick tock where her  popularity has gone off the charts this  year the videos are one of a kind  blending deeply personal observations  with stunning food preparation inspired  by her parents struggles growing up in  north korea and her own move to a  plant-based diet now molinaro has  released her first cookbook the korean  vegan in which she mixes up many  traditional korean meals like short ribs  wings and fish stews  we sat down with her in chicago for the  full experience great cooking  and conversation  we're roasting the kochikaru we're  getting a little cook on it so what are  we making we're making sundubu which is  silken tofu stew  it is probably the first thing that my  mom ever taught me to make joanne lee  molinaro can make just about anything  you want  as long as it doesn't include any dairy  or meat you know for plant-based food  you're trying to find as many different  ways to add flavor body and depth that  you possibly can molinaro's story began  half a world away as she details in her  new book the korean vegan which includes  the emotional story of her family's  journey  several of molinaro's ancestors  including her parents were all born in  what is now known as north korea when i  first discovered that my parents were  born in quote north korea i was really  embarrassed i was very ashamed and i  don't even know why i think it just  evinces my complete ignorance  and  total lack of interest and really  understanding  my family's history or my country's  history  there was no reason for me to be ashamed  of where my parents were born but i was  so i just lied i used to tell people i  don't know my parents were born in seoul  you know molinero's parents made it to  the u.s in the mid-1970s  she went on to become a successful trial  lawyer  then five years ago she made maybe the  biggest decision of her life you went  vegan in the first place why  the most honest answer was because my  then boyfriend now husband he wanted to  go vegan and for a while i was like well  you're on your own buddy i'm not doing  that with you i can't give up my kimchi  and i'm not going to give up my progogy  on your own but there is fellowship in  eating and i was worried that if i  didn't eat the same foods that he was  eating we would lose that and so i  figured well just give it a try see what  happens like it's not going to kill you  it's the man's fault yeah yeah it's  always the man's fault molinaro started  a food blog that same year and began  posting videos the following year  they are like nothing else online ever  since i was little my mom made it very  clear to me that she was embarrassed  about certain aspects of our culture  beautifully shot demonstrations narrated  by molinero not really describing a  cooking process when non-korean friends  came over after school she would always  apologize in advance for the smell but  instead her feelings on life work and  society in general for the record i'm  done apologizing and so is my mom i  think what i'm trying to do is to create  a memorable experience  and memorable experiences are created by  some kind of emotion mine is  sentimentality nostalgia those types of  things i'm not really good at comedy  so i focus on my strengths you say at  the dinner table your family didn't  spill their guts  they filled them yeah we were very quiet  during dinner it's basically the only  sounds you heard were the ones that i  described like slurping  and like clink clink clink you know with  those chopsticks and stuff sometimes i  would actually bring like a book  or have my homework like on the table  with me while i was eating they say that  time heals all wounds they're lying some  wounds will never ever heal in the last  year molinero has picked up nearly three  million followers on tick tock that  success has allowed her to transition  away from legal work to focus on cooking  almost full time  the feast she laid out for us is just a  small sample of that what you have here  is rice right okay so that's pop and  this is you know kind of my favorite mix  of rice you've got here brown rice sweet  brown rice pearl barley as well as a  little bit of black forbidden rice which  is why it comes in that lovely color and  then to the right of that you're going  to have some silken tofu stew or  sundubuchike this is the delicious spicy  stew that you helped me to make  and then we've got here mushroom kirby  got here braised tofu cucumber kimchi  chapchae  kiran mari which is a korean style  omelette some kale with some fermented  soy bean dressing we've got here some  soy glazed potatoes and then some  pickled perilla leaf obviously pichu  kimchi and then some perilla leaf  focaccia  so what do we do first you know you  really can eat whatever it's like mix in  like yeah go wherever i mean that's the  thing i love about do i bring this into  the right or like you can my husband  just like scoops it into the rice okay  um i like it separate because i like to  enjoy all the flavors on their own but a  lot of people eat it just the way you  described  delicious oh good molinero has quickly  become one of the world's most zealous  advocates for a plant-based diet  insisting not only that it's healthier  and more sustainable than eating dairy  and meats but also they can be just as  flavorful i mean the thing here is that  like  there's no meat  but  stuff tastes meaty  [Laughter]  right yeah there's a heartiness to  korean food that i think  even i when i first went vegan i was  like well then i guess i just have to  eat salads all the time you know that's  what i thought no this is like there's  no salad on this entire table  do you think people are getting more and  more comfortable with the idea of going  vegan absolutely  i mean i feel like every day there's  another news story about a new  plant-based product a new plant-based  restaurant or even if it means switching  out you know one meal a week with a  plant-based alternative i think that's  wonderful  where do you think the korean vegan goes  next  that's a good question jeff  i think that right now  i would love to  write more i love writing and i think  also making foods that look and taste  this amazing more accessible to people  is important to me whether that's you  know proliferating more recipes through  cookbooks or a blog or videos ultimately  the korean vegan will go wherever  compassion and empowerment leads it to  go  my first time making tofu stew it was  enjoyable it looked good we have come a  long way in 30 years it is a beautifully  written book and her videos really are  one of a kind  well

